\title{The Topology of Symmetric Argumentation:\\
Facets, Grounded Extensions, and the Euler Bridge}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 12, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the interplay between the \emph{semantics} of abstract argumentation
frameworks in the sense of Dung and the \emph{combinatorial topology} of their
associated conflict-free complex $K(\AF)$. In a general framework the natural
guess that the Euler characteristic of $K(\AF)$ equals
$\#(\text{preferred}) - \#(\text{grounded})$ is false. We isolate the class of
\textbf{symmetric} frameworks --- those in which attacks are mutual --- and prove
that here the gap between the combinatorial and the semantic worlds closes
completely. Our central structural result is that in a symmetric framework every
conflict-free set is admissible, because each argument defends itself;
consequently the preferred extensions are exactly the \emph{facets} (maximal
faces) of $K(\AF)$, and the grounded extension is exactly the set of unattacked
arguments. Specializing to the complete conflict graph on $n$ arguments, whose
complex is $n$ isolated points, we obtain an exact \textbf{Euler bridge}: for
$n \geq 1$ the Euler characteristic of $K(\AF)$ equals the number of preferred
extensions, both equal to $n$. We show the hypothesis $n \geq 1$ is sharp by
exhibiting the failure at $n = 0$. These results replace the refuted naive
identity with a precise topological reading of argumentation semantics on the
symmetric side of the theory, and identify the correct mathematical home of the
correspondence: the independence complex of the mutual-attack graph.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Abstract argumentation, introduced by Dung, models reasoning under conflict with
striking economy. An \emph{argumentation framework} is a pair $\AF = (A, R)$
where $A$ is a set of \emph{arguments} and $R \subseteq A \times A$ is an
\emph{attack relation}; we write $R\,a\,b$ for ``$a$ attacks $b$.'' From this
single relation Dung extracts a rich family of \emph{semantics} --- criteria for
which sets of arguments a rational agent may accept together --- that have become
foundational in non-monotonic reasoning, multi-agent systems, and formal models
of debate.

A separate observation gives the theory a geometric flavour. The
\emph{conflict-free} sets of a framework --- those containing no internal attack
--- form a downward-closed family, hence an \textbf{abstract simplicial
complex}, which we call the \textbf{conflict-free complex} $K(\AF)$. This invites
topological invariants: connectivity, homology, and the Euler characteristic. It
is natural to hope for a clean bridge between the two worlds, for instance a
formula expressing the Euler characteristic of $K(\AF)$ in terms of counts of
distinguished extensions. The most naive such formula is false.

This paper shows that on the \textbf{symmetric} side of the theory --- where the
attack relation satisfies $R\,a\,b \Rightarrow R\,b\,a$, the natural model of
two-sided disagreement --- a clean bridge survives, in a strong form. We prove:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Self-defense} (Theorem~\ref{thm:selfdefense}): in a symmetric
        framework every conflict-free set is admissible.
  \item \textbf{Facet identification} (Theorem~\ref{thm:facet}): the preferred
        extensions of a symmetric framework are exactly the maximal
        conflict-free sets, i.e.\ the facets of $K(\AF)$.
  \item \textbf{Grounded $=$ unattacked} (Theorem~\ref{thm:grounded}): the
        grounded extension of a symmetric framework is exactly the set of
        unattacked arguments.
  \item \textbf{Euler bridge} (Theorem~\ref{thm:euler}): for the complete
        conflict graph on $n \geq 1$ arguments,
        $\chi(K(\AF)) = \#(\text{preferred}) = n$; the bound $n \geq 1$ is sharp
        (Proposition~\ref{prop:sharp}).
\end{enumerate}
Together these results give the correct Euler/semantics correspondence for
symmetric frameworks and locate it precisely: $K(\AF)$ is the independence
complex of the (symmetric) attack graph, and the correspondence is a statement
about maximal independent sets.

\section{Preliminaries: frameworks and conflict-freeness}

Throughout, $A$ is a set (of arguments) and $R$ a binary relation (attack) on
$A$. We do not assume $A$ finite except where stated.

\begin{definition}[Conflict-free set]
A set $S \subseteq A$ is \emph{conflict-free} if no member of $S$ attacks
another: for all $a, b \in S$, $\neg\,R\,a\,b$.
\end{definition}

Conflict-freeness is monotone under passing to subsets: any subset of a
conflict-free set is conflict-free (removing arguments cannot create an attack).
This is the geometric backbone of the theory.

\begin{definition}[Conflict-free complex]
The \emph{conflict-free complex} $K(\AF)$ is the family of all conflict-free
subsets of $A$. Being downward closed, it is an abstract simplicial complex: its
faces are the conflict-free sets, its vertices the singletons $\{a\}$, its edges
the conflict-free pairs, and so on.
\end{definition}

Equivalently, defining the (symmetric) \emph{conflict graph} whose vertices are
the arguments and whose edges join arguments that attack each other in either
direction, $K(\AF)$ is precisely the \textbf{independence complex} of that graph:
its faces are the independent sets.

\section{Dung semantics}

\begin{definition}[Defense]
A set $S \subseteq A$ \emph{defends} an argument $a$ if every attacker of $a$ is
counter-attacked from $S$: for all $b$ with $R\,b\,a$, there exists $c \in S$
with $R\,c\,b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Admissible set]
$S \subseteq A$ is \emph{admissible} if it is conflict-free and defends each of
its members.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Characteristic / defense operator]
The \emph{defense operator} $F = F_R : \pow(A) \to \pow(A)$ is
$F(S) = \{\, a \in A : S \text{ defends } a \,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity]\label{lem:mono}
$F$ is monotone: if $S \subseteq T$ then $F(S) \subseteq F(T)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $S$ defends $a$, every attacker $b$ of $a$ has a counter-attacker
$c \in S \subseteq T$; hence $T$ defends $a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Preferred extension]
$S$ is a \emph{preferred extension} if it is a maximal admissible set: $S$ is
admissible and any admissible $T \supseteq S$ equals $S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Grounded extension]
By Knaster--Tarski, the monotone operator $F$ on the complete lattice $\pow(A)$
has a least fixed point. The \emph{grounded extension} is
$G := \lfp(F)$, with $F(G) = G$. It is obtained by iterating $F$ from
$\emptyset$, and is the \emph{skeptical} semantics.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Defense of nothing $=$ the unattacked]\label{lem:empty}
$F(\emptyset) = \{\,a : a \text{ has no attacker}\,\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $\emptyset$ defends $a$, then $a$ can have no attacker (there is no
counter-attacker in $\emptyset$). Conversely if $a$ has no attacker, the defense
condition holds vacuously.
\end{proof}

\section{Symmetric frameworks and self-defense}

\begin{definition}[Symmetric framework]
$\AF = (A, R)$ is \emph{symmetric} if $R\,a\,b \Rightarrow R\,b\,a$ for all
$a, b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Self-defense]\label{thm:selfdefense}
In a symmetric framework, every conflict-free set is admissible.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $S$ be conflict-free and $a \in S$. Let $b$ be any attacker of $a$, so
$R\,b\,a$. By symmetry $R\,a\,b$, and $a \in S$; thus $a$ is a member of $S$ that
attacks $b$, witnessing that $S$ counter-attacks $b$. Since $b$ was arbitrary,
$S$ defends $a$; and $a$ was arbitrary in $S$, so $S$ is admissible.
\end{proof}

The mechanism is that each argument \emph{defends itself}: an attacker is always
attacked back by its target. Admissibility, the obstruction separating semantics
from mere conflict-freeness in general, becomes automatic.

\begin{corollary}[Collapse]\label{cor:collapse}
In a symmetric framework, $S$ is admissible if and only if $S$ is conflict-free.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Admissible sets are conflict-free by definition; the converse is
Theorem~\ref{thm:selfdefense}.
\end{proof}

\section{Preferred extensions are facets}

\begin{definition}[Maximal conflict-free set / facet]
$S$ is \emph{maximal conflict-free} if it is conflict-free and any conflict-free
$T \supseteq S$ equals $S$. Equivalently, $S$ is a \emph{facet} of $K(\AF)$: an
inclusion-maximal face.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Facet identification]\label{thm:facet}
In a symmetric framework, $S$ is a preferred extension if and only if $S$ is a
maximal conflict-free set (a facet of $K(\AF)$).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) If $S$ is maximal admissible, it is conflict-free
(Corollary~\ref{cor:collapse}). Any conflict-free $T \supseteq S$ is admissible
(Theorem~\ref{thm:selfdefense}), so $T = S$. Hence $S$ is maximal conflict-free.

($\Leftarrow$) If $S$ is maximal conflict-free, it is admissible
(Theorem~\ref{thm:selfdefense}). Any admissible $T \supseteq S$ is
conflict-free, so $T = S$. Hence $S$ is maximal admissible, i.e.\ preferred.
\end{proof}

Thus a \emph{semantic} notion (preferred $=$ maximal credulous positions)
coincides with a \emph{topological} one (the facets of the independence complex),
and the preferred extensions are exactly the maximal independent sets of the
conflict graph.

\section{The grounded extension of a symmetric framework}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Ufix}
The set $U := \{\,a : a \text{ has no attacker}\,\}$ satisfies $F(U) = U$ in a
symmetric framework.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
($\subseteq$) Let $a \in F(U)$ and suppose $a$ has an attacker $b$. Since $U$
defends $a$, some $c \in U$ has $R\,c\,b$; by symmetry $R\,b\,c$, contradicting
$c \in U$. Hence $a \in U$. ($\supseteq$) If $a \in U$ the defense condition is
vacuous, so $a \in F(U)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:UsubG}
$U = F(\emptyset) \subseteq F(G) = G$, where $G = \lfp(F)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$U = F(\emptyset)$ is Lemma~\ref{lem:empty}. By monotonicity
(Lemma~\ref{lem:mono}), $F(\emptyset) \subseteq F(G) = G$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Grounded $=$ unattacked]\label{thm:grounded}
In a symmetric framework, the grounded extension equals the set of unattacked
arguments: $G = U$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$U$ is a fixed point of $F$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:Ufix}) and $G$ is the least fixed
point, so $G \subseteq U$; and $U \subseteq G$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:UsubG}. Hence
$G = U$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Euler bridge for the complete conflict graph}

\begin{definition}[Complete conflict graph]
For $n \in \mathbb{N}$, the \emph{complete conflict graph} $\KAF_n$ on
$\{0, \dots, n-1\}$ is defined by $R\,a\,b \iff a \neq b$. It is symmetric and
irreflexive.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Conflict-free $=$ subsingleton]\label{lem:cfsub}
In $\KAF_n$, $S$ is conflict-free iff $|S| \leq 1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Two distinct members $a \neq b$ attack each other; a set with at most one member
has no distinct pair.
\end{proof}

Geometrically, $K(\KAF_n)$ has $n$ vertices and no edges: it is $n$ isolated
points.

\begin{theorem}[Preferred $=$ singletons]\label{thm:prefsing}
For $n \geq 1$, the preferred extensions of $\KAF_n$ are exactly the singletons
$\{a\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:facet} the preferred extensions are the maximal
conflict-free sets, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:cfsub} the conflict-free sets are
$\emptyset$ and the singletons. Since $n \geq 1$, $\emptyset$ is not maximal,
while each singleton is; the maximal ones are exactly the singletons.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Count of preferred extensions]\label{cor:count}
For $n \geq 1$, $\#\{\,S : S \text{ preferred in } \KAF_n\,\} = n$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The preferred extensions are the singletons $\{a\}$, and $a \mapsto \{a\}$ is
injective, so there are exactly $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Euler characteristic]
For a finite simplicial complex with face set $K$,
\[
\chi(K) = \sum_{\emptyset \neq s \in K} (-1)^{\dim s}, \qquad \dim s = |s| - 1,
\]
equivalently $\chi(K) = \sum_{k \geq 0} (-1)^k f_k$ with $f_k$ the number of
$k$-dimensional faces (of size $k+1$).
\end{definition}

For $\KAF_n$ the only nonempty faces are the $n$ singletons, each of dimension
$0$, so $\chi(K(\KAF_n)) = n$.

\begin{theorem}[Euler bridge]\label{thm:euler}
For the complete conflict graph on $n \geq 1$ arguments,
\[
\chi\big(K(\KAF_n)\big) = \#\{\,S : S \text{ preferred}\,\} = n.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The left equality is the computation above ($K(\KAF_n)$ is $n$ isolated points).
The right equality is Corollary~\ref{cor:count}.
\end{proof}

Because the grounded extension of $\KAF_n$ is empty (every argument is attacked,
so $U = \emptyset$ and, by Theorem~\ref{thm:grounded}, $G = \emptyset$), the
correct statement is $\chi = \#(\text{preferred})$: an exact count of the maximal
independent sets by a topological invariant. The naive identity
$\chi = \#(\text{preferred}) - \#(\text{grounded})$ fails in general.

\begin{proposition}[Sharpness at $n = 0$]\label{prop:sharp}
The hypothesis $n \geq 1$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:euler} cannot be dropped.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
For $n = 0$ the argument set is empty; the only conflict-free set is $\emptyset$,
so $K(\KAF_0)$ is a single empty face with no nonempty faces, giving $\chi = 0$.
But $\emptyset$ is vacuously admissible and is the unique subset of the empty
set, hence the unique preferred extension. Thus
$0 = \chi \neq \#(\text{preferred}) = 1$.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{description}
  \item[Algorithm A (Enumerate conflict-free sets).] Iterate over all subsets
        $S \subseteq A$ and retain those with no internal attack; complexity
        $O(2^{|A|}|A|^2)$. The result is $K(\AF)$.
  \item[Algorithm B (Preferred extensions).] From $K(\AF)$, keep the
        inclusion-maximal faces; for symmetric frameworks these are the preferred
        extensions by Theorem~\ref{thm:facet}. For general frameworks, filter
        additionally by admissibility.
  \item[Algorithm C (Grounded extension).] Iterate $S_{k+1} = F(S_k)$ from
        $S_0 = \emptyset$ until stabilization; the limit is $G = \lfp(F)$,
        reached in at most $|A|$ steps.
  \item[Algorithm D (Euler characteristic).] Tally the face counts
        $(f_0, f_1, \dots)$ from $K(\AF)$ and return $\sum_k (-1)^k f_k$.
\end{description}

\section{Applications and interpretation}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:facet}, computing the preferred extensions of a symmetric
framework is exactly listing the maximal independent sets of the conflict graph,
so independence-complex combinatorics applies to argumentation semantics. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:grounded}, skeptical acceptance is trivial: an argument is
grounded-accepted iff nobody challenges it. Coarse invariants of $K(\AF)$ acquire
argumentative meaning: connected components measure fragmentation of a debate,
induced cycles correspond to circular disagreements, and the Euler characteristic
can count semantic objects outright (Theorem~\ref{thm:euler}). Finally the Euler
characteristic of $K(\AF)$ is that of the independence complex of a graph, so
existing combinatorial-topology results transfer directly.

\section{Discussion}

The naive Euler/semantics identity fails because admissibility --- the
self-defense condition defining preferred extensions --- is invisible to the
complex $K(\AF)$, which records only conflict-freeness. Symmetry removes exactly
this obstruction (Theorem~\ref{thm:selfdefense}), aligning the two worlds:
preferred $=$ facets (Theorem~\ref{thm:facet}), grounded $=$ isolated vertices
(Theorem~\ref{thm:grounded}), and, on the complete conflict graph, Euler
characteristic $=$ number of preferred extensions (Theorem~\ref{thm:euler}). The
sharpness result (Proposition~\ref{prop:sharp}) marks the boundary and the
reduced-vs-unreduced subtlety at the empty framework. The conceptual payoff is a
relocation of the problem to the independence complex of the mutual-attack graph.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{Facet theorem for all symmetric irreflexive frameworks.} For every
symmetric, irreflexive framework, the preferred extensions should be exactly the
maximal independent sets, and $\chi(K(\AF)) = \sum_k (-1)^{k+1} f_k$ with $f_k$
the number of independent sets of size $k$.

\paragraph{Connected-component decomposition.} If the conflict graph is a
disjoint union $G_1, \dots, G_m$, the preferred extensions should be unions of
one preferred extension per $G_i$, with $\chi$ multiplicative (reduced) and the
number of preferred extensions equal to $\prod_i \#(\text{preferred of } G_i)$.

\paragraph{$H_0$ counts the debate fragments.} The rank of $\tilde H_0(K(\AF))$
should equal one less than the number of connected components of the conflict
graph.

\paragraph{Circular disagreements are 1-cycles.} An induced odd cycle in the
symmetric attack graph should contribute a nontrivial class to $H_1(K(\AF))$.

\paragraph{Homology and further semantics.} Define the simplicial chain complex
of $K(\AF)$ and study $H_\ast$ directly, with the Euler--Poincar\'e formula
$\chi = \sum_n (-1)^n \dim H_n$; integrate stable and ideal semantics and the
chain grounded $\subseteq$ ideal $\subseteq$ preferred into this picture.

